WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.880
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, next speaker is

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.320
Hai Le from Washington
University, St. Louis?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:11.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:12.590
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.875
&gt;&gt; And he's going to talk
about Precision-Recall.

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:17.785
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone. My name is

00:00:17.785 --> 00:00:20.535
Hai from Washington
University in St. Louis.

00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:26.935
This is Jaguar with my advisor
Professor Brendan Juba.

00:00:26.935 --> 00:00:31.080
So the problem that we
worked on is Class Imbalance.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.560
So, we all know that
class imbalance is a situation

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.950
when one class severely
out-represent other classes.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.930
So some practitioners claim that

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.000
class imbalance make
it harder for them

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.615
for classifying imbalanced data,

00:00:47.615 --> 00:00:49.760
but existing learning theory do

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.855
not refer to class imbalance.

00:00:51.855 --> 00:00:54.835
So although there are

00:00:54.835 --> 00:00:57.270
many methods that
have been proposed

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.875
to correct the imbalance like

00:00:59.875 --> 00:01:02.830
various sampling method or
the cost sensitive method,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.490
but we have a question,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.040
do those methods really help?

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.215
So in our work,

00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:11.460
first we derived
the relationship

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.160
between precision-recall
and accuracy,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.780
and then we claim that
the large data set

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.620
is the only cure
for class imbalance.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.675
So we proved that both
theoretically and empirically.

00:01:23.675 --> 00:01:28.715
Further related work in
this problem, Reader et al.

00:01:28.715 --> 00:01:31.490
empirically saw that
every metric except

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.385
for accuracy depends
on the class imbalance.

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:37.340
Alvarez also discovered
the relationship

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.030
between precision,
recall and accuracy.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.275
However, he didn't
like interpret

00:01:42.275 --> 00:01:45.985
the consequence of
that relationship.

00:01:45.985 --> 00:01:49.380
So first, let's look
at our derivation for

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.985
the relationship between
precision-recall and accuracy.

00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:54.865
So our theorem is,

00:01:54.865 --> 00:01:58.255
suppose D is a distribution
over example with

00:01:58.255 --> 00:02:02.310
Boolean label and
base positive rate mu,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.750
and suppose we have

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.575
a classifier h with
precision greater than 0.5.

00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:13.015
Then our theorem is
define epsilon maximum,

00:02:13.015 --> 00:02:15.240
precision and recall error,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.305
then epsilon max has to satisfy,

00:02:18.305 --> 00:02:22.310
mu epsilon max is less or
equal than epsilon accuracy

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.600
and less or equal than
three times mu epsilon max.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.220
So, in general, precision
and recall is invalid where

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.690
the accuracy is
scaled by the factor

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.035
of three times mu
the positive rate.

00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:42.350
So the consequence
of this theorem is,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.465
if we integrate with
the VC of the accuracy,

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:48.200
we can conclude that with

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.510
probability at least
one minus delta,

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.510
at least one over

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.310
mu epsilon max times
d plus log one over

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.395
delta example are
necessary and sufficient to

00:03:01.395 --> 00:03:03.420
achieve precision and recall

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.770
greater than
one minus epsilon max,

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.410
where d is the VC dimension
of the classifier.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:16.080
So the precision of
the term one over mu is very

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.320
important because it lead
to the consequence that

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.475
when the objective is
high precision and high recall,

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:27.485
class imbalance does
impose a cost on learning.

00:03:27.485 --> 00:03:29.430
That means that in order

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.625
to achieve high
precision and recall,

00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:34.180
it demand that the size

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.835
of the training data scale
by the imbalance.

00:03:36.835 --> 00:03:40.070
So we conclude that using

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:43.080
large training data size is
the cure for imbalanced data,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.110
because it is what controls
the generalization error.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.525
So, let's look at some case
study when we really need

00:03:51.525 --> 00:03:54.200
high precision because remember

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.210
when I talked about our theorem,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.630
we made the assumption that

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.310
the precision is
greater than 0.5.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.190
So, we look at

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.150
some case study when we
really need high precision.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.630
So the first example is

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.925
the ICU alarm system for
predicting cardiac arrest.

00:04:10.925 --> 00:04:12.885
So because cardiac arrest is

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.505
a very rare event and
is very expensive,

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:20.120
so we really want that
when the ICU alarm system

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.590
is triggered the patient

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.970
actually need to
transfer to the ICU,

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.920
so indeed we want
high precision.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.910
Another example that we

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.050
want to talk to is
the machine translation example.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:36.800
So, in this one we also need

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.780
really high precision
and recall because you

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.410
can have high accuracy but

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.030
low recall if you predict
the rare word and never use,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:52.500
but you can also have higher
accuracy but low precision

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.035
but in this case
the choice that you use of

00:04:55.035 --> 00:04:59.400
the words in the translation
might be meaningless.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.090
So, Brants et al.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.260
has a system that's
so continual improvement as

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:07.700
the training data increases

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.730
up to 100 billion of examples.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.720
So, it's not too

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.980
clear why he needs
so much amount of data,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.925
so we did lay
some investigation and

00:05:17.925 --> 00:05:19.940
we observed that most of

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.095
the sentences
contains rare words.

00:05:22.095 --> 00:05:23.580
And the frequency of

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.450
the rarest word in the target
sentence would determine

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.800
the amount of data
that are required to

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.610
reliably translate the sentence.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.180
So we did an experiment on

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.845
the New York Times corpus
and we observed that at

00:05:39.845 --> 00:05:43.825
least 1.5 million
example are necessary

00:05:43.825 --> 00:05:50.140
to correctly learn
55% of the sentence.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:53.535
So, for the experiment part,

00:05:53.535 --> 00:05:55.900
the aim of this experiment part

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:57.850
is to verify our conclusion

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.840
that using large
training data size is

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.490
necessary to cope
with class imbalance.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.030
So in order to do that,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.315
we are going to compare

00:06:06.315 --> 00:06:09.440
the performance of
different techniques for fixing

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.365
class imbalance versus
no modification version

00:06:13.365 --> 00:06:16.615
but trained on
larger training size.

00:06:16.615 --> 00:06:19.500
So the data set that we use in

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.655
this experiment is
drug discovery data set with

00:06:22.655 --> 00:06:25.880
about 1 million negative label

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.765
and have chose like
62,000 positive label.

00:06:29.765 --> 00:06:33.020
So in the first part
of the experiment,

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.300
first we stratify the data into

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.795
20 folds with about
50,000 samples each.

00:06:42.795 --> 00:06:44.320
And then we select one fold

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.570
and we create different model by

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:52.720
re-sampling this using
different imbalance techniques,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.360
like oversampling,
undersampling, SMOTE,

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.760
and then we change
the model using KNN.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.520
For the rest of the data,

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.660
we just change using KNN and we

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.350
repeat the experiment 200

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:10.410
times and compare
the performance.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:18.730
So you can see, for
the no modification version,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.010
when trained on
a large data size

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.230
we have significant
higher precision and

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:26.190
recall than the model

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:29.530
that use different
imbalance techniques.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.900
In the second part
of the experiment,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:39.580
we compare the performance
by different data

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:46.935
set size from 100,000
to 500,000 samples.

00:07:46.935 --> 00:07:52.865
You can see that
the precision and recall

00:07:52.865 --> 00:07:56.450
scale when the size of the data

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:01.510
set increase in
the no modification version.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:05.520
So, to conclude, we

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.270
conclude that it is
not possible to achieve

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.530
high precision and recall under

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.610
severe class imbalance unless

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.030
you have a certain
amount of data.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.280
And we claim that
the method for correcting

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.310
class imbalance
don't help and you

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.520
shouldn't trust classifiers on

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.915
typical size data set with
high imbalance because

00:08:27.915 --> 00:08:30.960
it is very similar to
the situation that you

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.130
have low accuracy on
a small data set size.

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.380
So, our advice, when you

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.000
have to deal with
class imbalance

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.905
but you don't have enough data,

00:08:41.905 --> 00:08:44.590
our advice is to rather than

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.820
trying different techniques
for correcting the imbalance,

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.300
you try to explore

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:52.080
some prior knowledge
about the domain like,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.310
for example, for
the machine translation example

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:56.575
that I talked before,

00:08:56.575 --> 00:09:01.600
you can try to look
for the definition of

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.740
the word in the dictionary
rather than trying

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.615
different techniques for
correcting imbalance.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:14.690
That's all on my talk.
Thank you, everyone.

